[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :actor/wave 2, :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:apply-physical-restraint :use-force :authorize-use-of-force :impose-disciplinary-sanction :impose-solitary-confinement :place-in-segregation :revoke-privileges :restrict-movement :restrict-inmate-movement :determine-confinement-conditions :authorize-cell-extraction :execute-cell-extraction], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["adr-2790003355-cloud-itonami-isco-3355-police-inspectors-detectives-coverage" "adr-2792005412-cloud-itonami-isco-5412-police-officers-coverage" "adr-2799005411-cloud-itonami-isco-5411-firefighters-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["apply the physical restraint" "applied the physical restraint" "use force on the inmate" "used force on the inmate" "use force on the incarcerated person" "used force on the incarcerated person" "authorize the use of force" "authorized the use of force" "impose the disciplinary sanction" "imposed the disciplinary sanction" "impose solitary confinement" "imposed solitary confinement" "place in solitary confinement" "placed in solitary confinement" "place in segregation" "placed in segregation" "revoke the privileges" "revoked the privileges" "revoke inmate privileges" "revoked inmate privileges" "restrict movement privileges" "restricted movement privileges" "restrict the inmate's movement" "restricted the inmate's movement" "authorize the cell extraction" "authorized the cell extraction" "execute the cell extraction" "executed the cell extraction" "determine the confinement conditions" "determined the confinement conditions" "change the confinement conditions" "changed the confinement conditions" "身体拘束を実施した" "有形力を行使した" "実力を行使した" "懲罰を科した" "独居拘禁を科した" "隔離拘禁を科した" "特権を剥奪した" "移動を制限した" "拘禁条件を変更した"], :adr/id 2799005413, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-facility-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-facility-record :schedule-staff-operation :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-facility-record :schedule-staff-operation :coordinate-supply-order :flag-facility-concern}, :auto #{:log-facility-record :schedule-staff-operation :coordinate-supply-order}}}", :actor/domain :public-safety/facility-equipment-and-scheduling-documentation-and-logistics-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-facility-record :schedule-staff-operation :flag-facility-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:officer-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :facility-basis :incarcerated-person-conclusion-forbidden :staff-operation-content-forbidden :weapon-restraint-item-forbidden :scope-exclusion], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "5413", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-facility-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2799005413: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 5413 Prison Guards facility/equipment/scheduling documentation/administrative-logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 5413\n-- Prison Guards** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"5413\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"5413\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Prison Guards\" -- confirming the\npremise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was\n`:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5413` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding, and again immediately before repo creation). This ADR and\nits companion registry edit promote the `5413` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This is one of the highest-stakes occupation actors built in this\nfleet's session, alongside its concurrent sibling ISCO-08 5412 (Police\nOfficers).** Prison Guards have direct legal authority to physically\nrestrain incarcerated persons, use force (including in cell-extraction\nand use-of-force incidents), control movement/privileges, and impose\ndisciplinary sanctions -- and, unlike most other high-stakes actors\nbuilt in this fleet, the population subject to those decisions is a\n**captive population with limited ability to resist wrongful\ntreatment**, which is a distinctly more severe risk shape than e.g. an\nordinary citizen interacting with a benefits office or a police\ndetective's case file. The controlling precedent is **ADR-2607152500**\n(Wave4 rollout start amendment), which established that decisions\ndirectly touching a person's safety, dignity or subsistence must always\nexclude the closed op allowlist and always escalate to a human, not\nmerely be gated behind a confidence threshold. The concurrent sibling\n`cloud-itonami-isco-3355` (Police Inspectors and Detectives, built\nearlier in this same session) applied that guardrail to an\ninvestigative-authority context (arrest/search/charge/guilt). This ADR\napplies the identical guardrail shape to a domain with an even more\nsevere failure mode given the captive-population vulnerability: a\nwrongful arrest can be an irreversible deprivation of physical liberty,\nbut a wrongful use-of-force or disciplinary action against someone\nALREADY incarcerated, who has essentially no ability to walk away or\ncall for outside help in the moment, compounds that severity. The\nguardrail here is therefore held to at least the same strictness as\n`cloud-itonami-isco-3355`'s, with an additional domain-specific\ndefense-in-depth check (a HARD, cost-independent block on any\nweapon/restraint-device procurement item) that has no analogue in the\n3355 reference because that domain's supply-coordination op never\ntouched physical force-projection equipment.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set). The\nreference mirrored for the closed-allowlist + registered-AND-verified\nprovenance + content-based scope-exclusion SAFETY PATTERN specifically\nis\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3355`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3355)\n(Police Inspectors and Detectives) -- both independently re-cloned and\nre-read in full in this session, adapted substantially to the\nuse-of-force/physical-restraint/disciplinary-sanction/confinement-\ncondition guardrail described below rather than following either\nreference's domain shape directly.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5413` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for prison guards, following\nthe SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Facility Ops Advisor ⊣\nFacilityOpsGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- this is a different library coordinate from\nthe `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **facility/equipment/scheduling\ndocumentation and administrative-logistics coordination ONLY, never\ncustodial or enforcement authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising ANY of: a\nuse-of-force decision, a physical-restraint decision, a decision to\nimpose disciplinary sanctions (solitary confinement, privilege\nrevocation, etc.), a decision restricting an incarcerated person's\nmovement/access, or any decision affecting an incarcerated person's\nconditions of confinement. These are not merely gated behind\nescalation -- they are structurally absent from the op-allowlist\nentirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-facility-record` -- non-custodial facility/equipment readiness\n  data entry (security-system/equipment condition metadata only --\n  equipment id, system, condition, timestamp -- NEVER an incident\n  conclusion about, or identification of, a specific incarcerated\n  person)\n- `:schedule-staff-operation` -- administrative staff shift/training\n  scheduling proposal (never a use-of-force/restraint/cell-extraction\n  operational plan)\n- `:flag-facility-concern` -- surface an equipment/security-system/\n  staffing concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- non-weapon facility-equipment\n  procurement proposal (never weapons/restraint devices, regardless of\n  cost)\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**: a\nuse-of-force decision, a physical-restraint decision, a disciplinary\nsanction, a movement/access restriction, or any confinement-condition\ndecision over an incarcerated person. These are **structurally absent\nfrom the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation --\nan op such as `:apply-physical-restraint` cannot even be proposed by\nthe reference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of\nthe four allowlist ops, driven by a `case` expression with no default\nbranch producing any other op shape), and if any code path (a forged\nproposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such\nan op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowed`)\nrejects it unconditionally and permanently. Verified two ways: (a) unit\ntests directly against `governor/check` for twelve concretely-named\nforbidden op fixtures (`facilityops.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:apply-physical-restraint`, `:use-force`, `:authorize-use-of-force`,\n`:impose-disciplinary-sanction`, `:impose-solitary-confinement`,\n`:place-in-segregation`, `:revoke-privileges`, `:restrict-movement`,\n`:restrict-inmate-movement`, `:determine-confinement-conditions`,\n`:authorize-cell-extraction`, `:execute-cell-extraction`), and (b) an\nEND-TO-END test that drives each of those same twelve ops through a\nROGUE advisor and the FULL `langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the\ngovernor's pure check function), proving the StateGraph itself always\nresolves to `:hold` with zero records committed even when the advisor\nnode itself is compromised\n(`facilityops.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Eight HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Officer unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve to\n   an independently `:registered?`/`:verified?` officer record. Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the officer is. This directly\n   implements the task's \"officer/facility record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action\" invariant for the officer\n   half; the officer record itself carries `:facility-id`, so officer\n   verification and facility assignment are checked together, not as\n   two separate entity lookups.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in an `:apply-physical-restraint`/`:use-force`/`:impose-\n   disciplinary-sanction`/`:restrict-inmate-movement`-shaped op.\n4. **Facility-record basis** -- `:log-facility-record`,\n   `:schedule-staff-operation` and `:coordinate-supply-order` must cite\n   a REGISTERED AND VERIFIED facility that the proposing officer is\n   themselves assigned to (`:missing-facility-id` / `:unknown-facility`\n   / `:facility-unverified` / `:facility-mismatch`). This is the second\n   half of the task's \"officer/facility record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action\" invariant.\n   `:flag-facility-concern` is deliberately EXEMPT from this check --\n   it is the channel by which a concern may be surfaced before facility\n   paperwork is complete, so it must not be blocked on the very\n   facility record it exists to help flag.\n5. **Incarcerated-person-conclusion forbidden** -- `:log-facility-\n   record` is non-custodial facility/equipment readiness metadata ONLY\n   (equipment id, system, condition, timestamp). Any proposal carrying\n   a field that identifies or draws a conclusion about a specific\n   incarcerated person (`:incarcerated-person-id`, `:inmate-id`,\n   `:person-of-concern`, `:conclusion`, `:finding`,\n   `:disciplinary-conclusion`, `:disciplinary-finding`,\n   `:behavioral-assessment`, `:behavioral-determination`,\n   `:incident-conclusion`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor\n   never records anything about a specific incarcerated person, only\n   the physical condition of facility equipment and systems. This check\n   is UNIQUE to this ADR relative to the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355`\n   reference's `:evidentiary-interpretation-forbidden` shape, adapted\n   from \"never an evidentiary conclusion\" to \"never a conclusion about\n   a specific incarcerated person.\"\n6. **Staff-operation-content forbidden** -- `:schedule-staff-operation`\n   is shift/training scheduling logistics ONLY. Any proposal carrying\n   operational/tactical content (`:force-plan`, `:restraint-plan`,\n   `:extraction-plan`, `:tactical-plan`,\n   `:use-of-force-authorization`, `:disciplinary-directive`,\n   `:cell-extraction-plan`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor\n   never plans, records or authorizes a use-of-force, restraint or\n   cell-extraction operation, only administrative shift/training\n   calendars.\n7. **Weapon/restraint-device supply forbidden** -- `:coordinate-supply-\n   order` is non-weapon facility-equipment procurement ONLY. Any\n   proposal whose `:item`/`:item-category` names a weapon or\n   physical-restraint device (`facilityops.governor/weapon-restraint-\n   item-terms`: firearm/gun/pistol/rifle/shotgun, taser/stun gun/stun\n   belt/conductive energy weapon, baton, handcuff, leg iron, shackle,\n   restraint chair, restraint device, waist chain, pepper/OC spray,\n   chemical agent, less-lethal, flash bang, rubber bullet, beanbag\n   round, capture net, ammunition -- matched case- and\n   separator-insensitively so a keyword- or slug-shaped\n   `:item-category` such as `:restraint-device` still matches) is a\n   HARD, PERMANENT block, independent of and in addition to the\n   cost-threshold escalation (check 9 below) -- **this is not merely\n   gated by cost, it never auto-commits or escalates to \"just sign\n   off\", it is unconditionally refused regardless of price.** This\n   check has NO analogue in the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` reference\n   (that domain's forensic/office-equipment supply op never touched\n   force-projection equipment) and is the single largest structural\n   addition this ADR makes over its safety-pattern template, directly\n   implementing the task's explicit instruction that\n   `:coordinate-supply-order` \"must additionally hard-block any\n   weapon/restraint-device procurement item, not just gate it.\"\n8. **Scope exclusion** -- independent of the op-allowlist check and\n   checks 5/6/7, any proposal whose free text (`:rationale` or `:note`)\n   names a finalization/execution action for applying physical\n   restraint, using force, imposing a disciplinary sanction, or\n   restricting an incarcerated person's movement/confinement conditions\n   (e.g. \"apply the physical restraint\", \"authorize the use of\n   force\", \"impose the disciplinary sanction\", \"restrict movement\n   privileges\", \"authorize the cell extraction\", \"determine the\n   confinement conditions\", plus Japanese equivalents) is a HARD,\n   PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally on content. This is\n   defense-in-depth on top of checks 3/5/6/7: even if a future change\n   somehow widened the allowlist or an allowed op's field shape,\n   content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   custodial-authority/enforcement language wherever it appears in free\n   text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-facility-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless\n  of confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an observation suggesting a\n  facility needs human corrections officer/supervisor attention -- it\n  never resolves the observation itself, and the robot's role ends at\n  \"here is the readiness/concern data,\" never \"here is what I\n  recommend doing about an incarcerated person.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (500, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs\n  human sign-off, independent of confidence, for a NON-WEAPON item\n  only (a weapon/restraint-device item never reaches this gate at all,\n  it is HARD-blocked by check 7 above regardless of cost). This is an\n  ESCALATION, not a hard block: an over-budget non-weapon facility\n  order (e.g. a replacement generator) is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  use-of-force/restraint/disciplinary/confinement-condition attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"restraint\", \"sanction\", \"privileges\") can accidentally match\ninside the mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly\nthe risk this vertical's own vocabulary (every proposal necessarily\ndiscusses facility/equipment/officer concepts to do its documentation\njob) could trigger if phrased carelessly. `facilityops.governor/scope-\nexcluded-terms` is phrased exclusively as finalization/execution\nACTIONS (e.g. \"apply the physical restraint\", \"impose the\ndisciplinary sanction\", \"restrict movement privileges\" -- the exact\nthree example phrases given in the task's guidance on this known bug\nclass), never bare nouns, and `facilityops.governor-test/default-mock-\nadvisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated\nregression test asserting every op in the closed allowlist (including\nboth the facility-id-present and facility-id-nil variants of\n`:flag-facility-concern`), driven through the default mock advisor,\nnever trips `:scope-excluded`, `:op-not-allowed`, or (a check unique to\nthis actor) `:weapon-restraint-item-forbidden`. The mock advisor's own\ndefault rationale template (\"documented <op> for facility <id>\" /\n\"documented <op> (no facility yet -- concern intake)\") was written to\navoid any bare-noun collision by construction. Green on first run in\nthis session after one unrelated fix (see Consequences) -- the\nscope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never tripped during\ndevelopment.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"5413\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5413` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-5413`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented count\n  bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately before\n  the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  batch, including the concurrent ISCO 5411/5412 siblings).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 51 tests, 172 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/counts`\n  and in the task report, independently re-verified against a\n  brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just the local\n  working copy).\n- One implementation bug was found and fixed during development, NOT\n  weakened around: the initial `weapon-restraint-item?` check matched\n  `:item-category` values against space-separated terms (e.g.\n  \"restraint device\") via a plain case-insensitive substring match,\n  which silently failed to match a hyphen- or underscore-separated\n  category value such as `:restraint-device` (a plausible real-world\n  keyword/slug shape for a categorical field). Fixed by normalizing\n  hyphens/underscores to spaces on both the input text and the\n  (already space-separated) term list before matching\n  (`facilityops.governor/normalize-item-text`), with a dedicated\n  regression test (`hard-on-weapon-restraint-supply-item-via-category`)\n  asserting the hyphenated form is still HARD-blocked.\n- `clj-kondo` lint of `src`/`test`: 0 errors, 0 warnings (ad hoc run;\n  this actor's `deps.edn` does not define a `:lint` alias, matching the\n  verified `cloud-itonami-isco-3313`/`-3355` references' own drift).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors this batch; only the central `kotoba-lang/\n  occupation` registry itself depends on it, to resolve\n  `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`/`SECURITY.md`/`GOVERNANCE.md`/`CONTRIBUTING.md`\n  say \"cloud-itonami organization\" for private-contact routing,\n  matching the factually-correct org name the repository is actually\n  published under (matching the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` sibling's\n  same fix, not the stale \"gftdcojp organization\" text still present\n  in the older `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` reference).\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-use-of-force`/`:approve-\n  restraint` that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops` (the\n  shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-\n  posting`, or the shape a naive reading of \"gate high-stakes actions\n  behind human approval\" might suggest): rejected outright per the\n  explicit task guidance, the `ADR-2607152500` precedent, and the\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` precedent -- an always-escalate op still\n  means the *capability* to apply force/restraint/discipline/confinement\n  changes exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For a decision this severe against a captive population with\n  limited ability to resist wrongful treatment, the capability itself\n  must not exist. The only op related to facility escalation is\n  `:flag-facility-concern`, which surfaces the need for human attention\n  and never itself decides, authorizes, or defers a custodial action\n  for later automatic execution.\n- Gating the weapon/restraint-device supply check behind the same\n  cost-threshold escalation as ordinary equipment (i.e. only requiring\n  human sign-off for an expensive restraint chair, auto-committing a\n  cheap one): rejected outright per the task's explicit instruction\n  that this check must be a HARD block regardless of cost, not a gate\n  -- a $1 handcuff order is exactly as structurally forbidden as a\n  $10,000 one, because the actor's role is documentation/logistics for\n  non-weapon equipment only, full stop, independent of price.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"restraint\"\n  \"sanction\" \"privileges\" \"force\"]`): rejected outright per the\n  fleet-known self-trip bug class described above, and per the task's\n  explicit worked examples of the correct action-phrase form -- this\n  actor's own domain necessarily discusses facility/equipment/\n  officer/scheduling concepts to do its documentation job, so a\n  bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own legitimate\n  proposals.\n- Merging `officer` and `facility` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the facility-mismatch check\n  requires two independently registered-AND-verified entities so that\n  a facility record an officer is not assigned to is caught even if\n  both records individually pass verification, matching the two-entity\n  provenance shape already established by `cloud-itonami-isco-3355`'s\n  officer+case-file pattern.\n- Allowing `:log-facility-record` to carry a coarse, non-identifying\n  \"area of concern\" field (e.g. a wing/unit-level flag distinct from\n  naming a specific incarcerated person): considered, but rejected for\n  this initial implementation -- even a coarse, unit-level field is a\n  plausible seed for scope creep toward person-level tracking over\n  time. Equipment/security-system condition metadata only for v1; a\n  follow-up ADR could reintroduce a narrowly-scoped, explicitly\n  non-identifying field if a legitimate administrative need is\n  demonstrated, with its own dedicated forbidden-keys guardrail.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-facility-record` /\n  `:schedule-staff-operation` / `:coordinate-supply-order` (no\n  forbidden-keys/forbidden-item content checks): rejected as\n  insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's certainty about the\n  *proposal it chose to make*, not whether that proposal smuggles\n  forbidden content; a high-confidence advisor could still misuse an\n  allowed op's field shape to carry a forbidden incarcerated-person\n  conclusion, a force/restraint operational plan, or a restraint-device\n  procurement item, which is exactly why checks 5/6/7 exist as\n  independent, content-based, permanent HARD blocks regardless of\n  confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-facility-record`** -- non-custodial facility/equipment\n   readiness metadata only (equipment id, system, condition,\n   timestamp). Cannot identify or draw a conclusion about a specific\n   incarcerated person (HARD-blocked by check 5,\n   `:incarcerated-person-conclusion-forbidden`, on a closed\n   forbidden-key list:\n   `:incarcerated-person-id`/`:inmate-id`/`:person-of-concern`/\n   `:conclusion`/`:finding`/`:disciplinary-conclusion`/\n   `:disciplinary-finding`/`:behavioral-assessment`/\n   `:behavioral-determination`/`:incident-conclusion`). Not a\n   use-of-force, restraint, disciplinary, or confinement-condition\n   decision in any respect -- it is a records-management action\n   indistinguishable in kind from a facilities technician's maintenance\n   log.\n2. **`:schedule-staff-operation`** -- shift/training scheduling\n   logistics only (operation type, proposed time, location). Cannot\n   carry a use-of-force/restraint/cell-extraction operational plan\n   (HARD-blocked by check 6, `:staff-operation-content-forbidden`, on a\n   closed forbidden-key list:\n   `:force-plan`/`:restraint-plan`/`:extraction-plan`/\n   `:tactical-plan`/`:use-of-force-authorization`/\n   `:disciplinary-directive`/`:cell-extraction-plan`). Does not plan,\n   authorize, or draw any conclusion about a custodial operation -- it\n   is a calendar action.\n3. **`:flag-facility-concern`** -- surfaces an equipment/security-\n   system/staffing concern to a human corrections officer/supervisor;\n   ALWAYS escalates (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally,\n   at every confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. Carries only\n   administrative trigger metadata (`:concern-type` from a closed\n   keyword set, `:note` free text subject to the same scope-exclusion\n   check as every other op's free text). This is the ONLY channel\n   through which a facility observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the human reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- non-weapon facility-equipment\n   procurement proposal (item, item category, cost, vendor). Cannot\n   name a weapon or physical-restraint device, regardless of cost\n   (HARD-blocked by check 7, `:weapon-restraint-item-forbidden`, on a\n   closed term list matched against both `:item` and\n   `:item-category`). Not an enforcement or custodial action of any\n   kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action for ordinary facility\n   equipment (e.g. medical supplies, uniforms, maintenance parts),\n   gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 500 always\n   escalates, for non-weapon items only) like any office/facility\n   procurement workflow.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na use-of-force decision, a physical-restraint decision, a decision to\nimpose disciplinary sanctions, a decision restricting an incarcerated\nperson's movement/access, or any decision affecting an incarcerated\nperson's conditions of confinement -- these five categories are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`facilityops.governor/closed-op-allowlist`), (b)\ntwelve concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures that are rejected at both\nthe governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph level via a rogue\nadvisor (`facilityops.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`facilityops.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`facilityops.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`facilityops.governor/out-of-scope?`,\n`facilityops.governor/scope-excluded-terms`), tested end-to-end through\na rogue advisor and the full compiled graph\n(`facilityops.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-rationale-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\nand (d) two additional content-shape guardrails plus one\ndomain-specific procurement-item guardrail, none of which have an\nanalogue in the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` template, preventing an\notherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance through its own\nfield shape (checks 5/6/7 above), all three independently proven\nend-to-end via a rogue advisor driving the full compiled StateGraph\n(`facilityops.actor-test/holds-an-incarcerated-person-conclusion-attempt`,\n`facilityops.actor-test/holds-a-staff-operation-content-attempt`,\n`facilityops.actor-test/holds-a-weapon-restraint-supply-order-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n", :adr/date "2026-07-17", :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 500}", :test/counts "{:total 51, :assertions 172, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :actor/coordination? true, :actor/name "Correctional Facility Equipment/Scheduling Documentation and Administrative-Logistics Coordination Practice", :db/id -1, :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :officer-verification :facility-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :incarcerated-person-conclusion-forbidden :staff-operation-content-forbidden :weapon-restraint-item-forbidden :rogue-advisor-end-to-end], :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-5413", :actor/weapon-restraint-item-terms ["firearm" "gun" "pistol" "rifle" "shotgun" "taser" "stun gun" "stun belt" "conductive energy weapon" "baton" "handcuff" "leg iron" "shackle" "restraint chair" "restraint device" "waist chain" "pepper spray" "oc spray" "chemical agent" "less lethal" "flash bang" "rubber bullet" "beanbag round" "capture net" "ammunition"], :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5413", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5413], :test/simulator :facilityops.actor-test, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-5413 — Prison Guards Facility/Equipment/Scheduling Documentation and Administrative-Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000 2607152500], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-5413}]